e the bike’s main mass couldn’t reach. --- ### What about “shifting your weight forward”? This part is also not about kilograms, but about **motorcycle geometry and metal positioning**: When a rider shifts forward, they typically roll the bike a few inches ahead. This moves: - The **engine block** (the single biggest metal lump) closer to the front wire of the loop. - The **front wheel rim** (metal) and possibly the **fork legs** into a different position relative to the loop wires. This repositioning can bring conductive metal into a more sensitive part of the field, or it might simply reposition the bike so the crankshaft or an aluminium frame member passes directly over one of the loop’s legs, where the eddy current coupling is stronger. The weight shift itself is irrelevant: you could stay seated and just roll the bike forward the same inch and get the same effect. The sensation of “shifting weight” gets conflated with the vehicle moving because they’re done together. --- ### The mental experiment that topples the weight theory Imagine you wore a backpack filled with 30 kg of lead shot (non-magnetic, poorly conductive) and stood on the detector loop. Nothing. Now place a single empty aluminium soda can directly over the wire cut. Still nothing, because it’s not a closed conductor cutting the field? Actually, a better test: a flat sheet of aluminium foil flat on the ground over the cut will create a very noticeable eddy current. A small metal object near the wire is what counts. If you could somehow place a tiny 50-gram steel washer precisely on the cut, it could increase the detector’s signal enough to be noticed, while an elephant standing dead centre would have zero effect. That’s the acid test: **it’s all about conductive material near the wire, not pressure**. --- ### Tying it all back to your dad’s truck Your dad’s truck has an enormous, low-hanging metal undercarriage: the truck frame, the engine, the transmission, multiple steel axles, the fifth-wheel cou